Repair the implementation below and return a complete JavaScript source file.

`serializeSafeRow(cells)` accepts an array of strings and returns one CSV row. Before CSV escaping, neutralize spreadsheet formulas: if a cell's first non-whitespace character is `=`, `+`, `-`, or `@`, prepend a single apostrophe to the entire cell. For this rule, whitespace means spaces, tabs, carriage returns, or line feeds. Then wrap a cell in double quotes if it contains a comma, double quote, carriage return, or line feed, and double every embedded double quote. Join cells with commas. Preserve all original characters otherwise. An empty array returns an empty string. Throw `TypeError` if the input is not an array of strings, and do not mutate it.

Use CommonJS and export exactly `{ serializeSafeRow }` with `module.exports`.

Known-bad source:
```javascript
'use strict';

function serializeSafeRow(cells) {
  if (!Array.isArray(cells) || !cells.every((cell) => typeof cell === 'string')) {
    throw new TypeError('cells must be an array of strings');
  }

  return cells.map((cell) => {
    let safe = cell;
    if (/^[\t\r\n ]*=/.test(safe)) {
      safe = `'${safe}`;
    }
    if (/[,"\r\n]/.test(safe)) {
      safe = `"${safe.replace(/"/g, '""')}"`;
    }
    return safe;
  }).join(',');
}

module.exports = { serializeSafeRow };
```

